Sent a self-contained code+data prompt to Gemini/ChatGPT and verified every
answer against this project's own primary sources before acting on it,
rather than accepting either on authority.

1. Fix the SLP1 r/f (र्ऋ vs रृ) transliteration collision using a ZWNJ
   marker (Gemini's proposal), but only after confirming empirically that
   it survives 4 consecutive re-joins, not just the 2 that exposed the
   original bug. samhita_join's _finish_consonant_then_vowel() now embeds
   the marker at the ambiguous boundary; a new strip_zwnj_markers() is
   called at every true final-output point (reconstruct_chain()'s return,
   and every place krama_engine.py turns a join surface into unit text or
   a length measurement) -- never at points that will be re-joined.
   Chain-reconstruction validation improves from 16/18 to 17/18 RV 1.1
   ardharcas matching DGE's attested text exactly.

2. Fix a second, independent bug in the diphthong (e/o/ai/au) + non-a-vowel
   branch: it kept the diphthong unchanged and merely appended a glide
   after it (e.g. "vane"+"indraH" -> an incorrect "vaneyindraH"), instead
   of replacing the diphthong with a/a/A/A + the glide per Panini 6.1.78
   (eco'yavAyAvaH). Confirmed against standard Sanskrit grammar
   independently of either AI's claim, not just asserted.

Checked two more of Gemini's Task-C findings ("avagraha collision",
"accent stripping fragility") empirically and found both already fixed by
the previous session's resolve_w1_compound fix -- reported as such rather
than "fixed" a second time.

Caught a real citation error even in an otherwise-correct answer: Gemini's
Q2 cited "RPr 1.74" for "vocative o is pragrhya"; this project's own
already-ingested corpus shows the actual sutra is 1.68 (1.74 is an
unrelated accent-placement rule). The substantive claim holds up against
this repo's own data; the citation didn't, and would have been repeated
uncritically otherwise. Q2's substance itself (does ordinary sandhi apply
between a word and "iti" for non-pragrhya Parigraha words?) is recorded as
a sharpened open question, not implemented on an inference.

Declined to adopt Gemini's Q3 citation (RPr 2.27, for RV 1.1.7's
visarga-before-aa gap) after checking it against this project's own
(uncertain-reading, not yet cross-checked) Patala 2 data and finding it
doesn't clearly support the specific claim made. That gap remains open.

Architecture doc sec.6.8 and PENDING.md record the full verify-then-act
trail: what was adopted, what was already fixed, what was checked and
rejected, and why in each case.

